anv: fix host image copies into emulated ASTC images; emulate ASTC 5x5 on Gfx9

Host image copy (VK_EXT_host_image_copy) into an image with an ASTC
emulation plane was broken in three ways:

 - On Gfx9 non-LP, the emulation plane keeps the ASTC format and only has
   void-extent denorms flushed.  vkCopyMemoryToImage still decoded the
   blocks to RGBA8 and wrote those bytes into the ASTC plane, so sampling
   returned the ASTC error colour.
 - The decode path passed the extent in blocks to _mesa_unpack_astc_2d_ldr,
   which expects texels, and its tile stepping mixed block and RGBA8 strides.
 - vkCopyImageToImage never reached its emulation branch (the API only
   addresses plane 0), so the sampled plane stayed uninitialised.  The dead
   branch also passed a VkFormat * where a util_format_description * was
   expected.

Replace both paths with write_emu_plane(), which converts strips of block
rows (flush or decode) and writes them in the plane's own units.

Gfx9 also hangs when ASTC 5x5 and CCS data share the sampler cache
(mesa#1892).  Iris fakes ASTC 5x5 on Gfx9 for that reason (MR 3251), but
ANV exposed native 5x5.  Decompress the two 5x5 formats into the emulation
plane on Gfx9 and keep native ASTC for other block sizes; the denorm flush
and decode modes are now chosen per image.  ANV_NATIVE_ASTC_5X5=true keeps
the old behaviour for comparison.

Tested on HD 620 (KBL GT2): GPU copy, host copy and host image-to-image of
ASTC 4x4/8x8/5x5 UNORM and sRGB match exact void-extent colours and the
hardware's native decode of random blocks (worst 0.0038 for emulated 5x5).

